Since the machine stayed dark both times, it must need two nexioms. Under "needs one", both nexioms would be among fokokol, dopefol and zitedu, and test 2 would have lit. So the machine needs two, and the pair must avoid both groups that stayed dark.

That leaves 7 possible pairs:
- {vobabar, zitedu}
- {roviti, zitedu}
- {durida, zitedu}
- {fokokol, zitedu}
- {dopefol, zitedu}
- {durida, fokokol}
- {durida, dopefol}

Next I'll test durida, fokokol and zitedu together, which splits these 7 pairs 3 to 4:
- **Lights (3 pairs):** {durida, zitedu}, {fokokol, zitedu} or {durida, fokokol}.
- **Stays dark (4 pairs):** {vobabar, zitedu}, {roviti, zitedu}, {dopefol, zitedu} or {durida, dopefol}.

Either way, two more tests will be enough to pin down the pair.

TEST: durida, fokokol, zitedu

The machine lit up with durida, fokokol and zitedu on it. Since it needs two nexioms, both must be among those three. That leaves 3 possible pairs:
- {durida, zitedu}
- {fokokol, zitedu}
- {durida, fokokol}

Next I'll test durida and zitedu on their own:
- **If it lights:** the nexioms are durida and zitedu.
- **If it stays dark:** the pair is {fokokol, zitedu} or {durida, fokokol}, and one more test will tell them apart.

TEST: durida, zitedu
